  "text": "Let's look at the given infinite product for $(x_n)$, which is\n\\[\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right).\\]\n\nFirst, we need to write out $x_n^2$ for small values of $n$, so we can see a pattern that we can trust:\n\nFor $n=1$,\n\\begin{align*}\nx_1 &= 2 \\\\\nx_1^2 &= 2^2 = 4.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFor $n=2$,\n\\begin{align*}\nx_1 &= 2 \\\\\nx_2 &= \\frac{1}{x_1} + x_1 \\tag{substituting $x_1$} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} + 2 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{4}{2} = \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\nx_2^2 &= \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25}{4}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe squares of $x_n$ give a pattern that we can continue:\n\\begin{align*}\nx_1^2 &= 4 \\\\\nx_2^2 &= \\frac{25}{4} \\\\\nx_3^2 &= x_2^4 - 2x_2^3 + x_2^2 \\\\\n&= \\(\\frac{625}{16} - 2\\frac{250}{16} + \\frac{25}{16}\\) \\\\\n&= \\frac{625 - 250 + 25}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{400}{16} \\\\\n&= 25 \\\\\nx_4^2 &= x_3^4 - 2x_3^3 + x_3^2 \\\\\n&= (25)^2 - 2(25)^2 + 25 \\\\\n&= 625 - 625 + 645 \\\\\n&= 645.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSubstituting this in,\n\\[\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right) = \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\rho}\\right),\\]\nwhere $\\rho$ is the $\\rho$ satisfying the equation $x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1 = 0$ which, in this case, is the\n\\[\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\rho}\\right) = 2.\\]\n\nTo summarise, we start with the initial value of $x_1$, then use that to calculate $x_2$, and so on. Each successive term in the\nis derived by the relation $x_k =\n\fform of the product.\n\nis,\n\n, then calculate the square-roots, and apply saw as we\ncan, which will produce a pattern more easily. Let's substitute some terms to check:\n\nWith some algebra work,\n\\[\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right) = 2.\\]\n\nSo we have\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]",
